nRelated to Insulin Resistance with Hyperglycemia: diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.61):]\n  Source: 2. Diagnosis and Classification of Diabetes: Standards of Care in Diabetes-2026. > Clinical Significance\n  Patients with hypoglycemia may present with shakiness, irritability, confusion, sweating, tachycardia, hunger, blurred vision, lightheadedness, clumsiness, or seizure activity. Impaired awareness of h\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: Approach to the Patient: Insulinoma. > History and Physical\n  Hypoglycemia manifests through a variety of symptoms. Symptoms related to sympathoadrenal activation may include palpitations, tremulousness, and diaphoresis. Severe hypoglycemia can lead to neuroglyc\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.67):]\n  Source: Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics of NPH Insulin in Type 1 Diabetes: The Importance of Appropriate Resuspension Before Subcutaneous Injection. > Contraindications\n  NPH insulin is contraindicated in hypersensitivity reactions. Patients may become hypersensitive to insulin formulation or any of the drug products. Allergic reactions to various allergens or additive\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Hypoglycemia secondary to factitious hyperinsulinism. > History and Physical\n  Physical examination is usually nonspecific. Vital signs may show tachycardia and hypothermia, and diaphoresis can be present on skin examination. In some patients, careful skin inspection for insulin\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  painful rash → necrolytic migratory erythema → Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma) with Associated Rash and Hyperglycemia\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Check for characteristic necrolytic migratory erythema (bilateral, annular, erythematous, blistering rash) to support glucagonoma diagnosis\n  malaise + worsening symptoms over week → progressive endocrine tumor syndrome → Hypercortisolism (Cushing's Syndrome) via Adrenal or Pituitary Tumor\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for signs of progressive tumor (e.g., new physical findings, cortisol overproduction patterns)\n  obesity + smoking history → metabolic syndrome → Insulin Resistance with Hyperglycemia\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess metabolic syndrome components (waist circumference, blood pressure, lipid panel) to confirm insulin resistance pathophysiology\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
